WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.702
-Amanda Gorman. I'm so excited
to have you here.

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.104
-I'm so excited to be here.
You know, this is my first

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:07.306
in-person
night talk show ever, so --

00:00:07.306 --> 00:00:08.541
-Is that right?
-Yes!

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:11.210
-Wow. I'm honored. I'm honored.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.545
You look gorgeous.
-Thank you.

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:15.915
-And I got to see you
the other night at the Met Gala,

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:17.783
and you looked gorgeous
that night as well, by the way.

00:00:17.783 --> 00:00:20.252
-Thank you. Oh, wow.
-Here's a photo of you.

00:00:20.252 --> 00:00:22.889
Can you talk me through it?
-Of course.

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:25.725
Well, the theme of the night
was basically American fashion,

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:27.893
and so I came to my team
with this idea

00:00:27.893 --> 00:00:29.896
of being a reimagined
Statue of Liberty,

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:31.997
but I really wanted
to leaning into kind of

00:00:31.997 --> 00:00:33.566
the poetry of the statue.

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:35.935
So, for example,
having a laurel crown,

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.170
which poets would wear
in ancient Greece.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:40.773
That's also where the title
"poet laureate" comes from,

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:43.109
and I was honored to be
a poet laureate in my life.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:45.512
Also bringing a bag
which looks like a book

00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:48.081
because I love reading
wherever I go

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:49.615
and making sure that I had,

00:00:49.615 --> 00:00:52.185
you know, a line from the poem
at the base of the statue.

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:55.121
So truly, like, try to walk
in the words of what it means

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.123
to welcome the world
to the United States.

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:58.858
-I loved it.
I thought it was so --

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:00.093
-Thank you.

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:02.528
-So much thought and so --
-Thank you.

00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:05.664
-I was telling you backstage
I was watching the inauguration.

00:01:05.664 --> 00:01:06.833
You blew us all away.

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:08.501
That was one of
the most impressive things

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:10.536
I've ever seen.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:12.372
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Knocked it out of the park.

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:15.241
-Thank you.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:19.846
-It was such an amazing job,
and I know that you got to meet

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:22.215
eventually Lin-Manuel Miranda,

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:24.984
who -- You kind of loosely
based a couple of the lines

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:26.886
in the poem
based off of "Hamilton."

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:28.954
Did you get a chance
to meet him and...?

00:01:28.954 --> 00:01:30.356
-Yeah, so, the incredible thing

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:33.259
is I actually met Lin
several years ago,

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:36.595
and I was so nervous,
I almost threw up on him.

00:01:36.595 --> 00:01:37.864
He does not know this.

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:40.733
But I wrote him a poem
of what I would say

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:45.704
if I could articulate words
to him in person, and he said,

00:01:45.704 --> 00:01:48.240
"I'm going to keep this
for, you know, I can say,

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.609
'I knew you when...'"

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:51.276
And, you know, basically
we're at the point now.

00:01:51.276 --> 00:01:53.212
He was like, "I knew you when!"
We've arrived there.

00:01:53.212 --> 00:01:54.413
He's been so great.

00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:55.949
-See? He's a magical guy.

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:57.183
I love that he did that.
-Yeah.

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:58.751
-Yeah, "I'm gonna keep this
'cause one day

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:00.185
I'm going to say,
'I knew you when.'"

00:02:00.185 --> 00:02:02.388
-Yeah, and he's, like,
three years later, on the dot.

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:04.123
-"I knew you when."
All right. That's great.

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:05.724
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:05.724 --> 00:02:07.693
I read somewhere -- Yeah.

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:09.529
I read somewhere
that you decided

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:11.197
that you're going to
run for president.

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.065
-Yes.
-In 2030...

00:02:13.065 --> 00:02:14.900
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.469
-In 2036.
-Yes.

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:19.605
-All right, so we have
some time, but you always knew

00:02:19.605 --> 00:02:21.207
that you wanted
to run for president?

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:25.144
-Yes, so, if you don't know,
2036 is the year

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:28.047
in which I will be old enough
to actually run for president,

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:29.816
but I've known that
for quite some time.

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:32.185
I was like 9 or 10,
and I came home. I was like,

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:35.822
"You know, Mom, I want to change
some things in this world,

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:37.824
so I'm going to
run for president."

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:39.358
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:39.358 --> 00:02:40.626
And...
-That's fantastic.

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:42.895
-And I have such
an incredible mom

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.997
that she knew
this wasn't a joke.

00:02:44.997 --> 00:02:47.433
She knew it wasn't
kind of an idle word

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:50.002
that I kind of tossed out
just for giggles.

00:02:50.002 --> 00:02:53.239
And so, you know, my family
has been so dedicated to it.

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:56.843
For example, if I'm ever
at a party with my twin sister

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:58.244
and people are taking photos,
she's like,

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:01.280
"Unh-uh-unh-unh. My sister
is running for president.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:02.681
Put your phone down."

00:03:02.681 --> 00:03:04.350
So they know
the campaign is real,

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.119
and, you know,
we just really orient

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:07.954
our lives living toward
best values

00:03:07.954 --> 00:03:10.356
because we know
at one point in time

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.257
I will aspire to the highest
seat of power in the country,

00:03:12.257 --> 00:03:14.260
so got to be prepared.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.262
-I think you're gonna do it.

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.564
I think you're
gonna go all the way.

00:03:18.564 --> 00:03:22.101
I want to talk about your book,
"Change Sings."

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:24.270
First of all,
congratulations on this.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.771
This went to --
-Thank you.

00:03:25.771 --> 00:03:28.574
-This shot up on Amazon
right after you did --

00:03:28.574 --> 00:03:29.975
It wasn't even out.
-Yeah.

00:03:29.975 --> 00:03:31.511
-They were like, "Oh, and
she might put this book out."

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:33.746
It was like, whoosh, number one.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:37.416
But anyways, it is --
I have two --

00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:40.820
I have two little girls, but --
And they have school,

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.089
and I know this is going to be
the best book in their school.

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:44.423
They're going to love this.

00:03:44.423 --> 00:03:47.793
It is such a great message
to every kid out there.

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:49.862
And I also love
the illustrations

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:52.131
and what happens with this poem.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:53.566
[ Fingers snapping ]
Yes, yes, yes.

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:55.168
-I'm a poet,
so I do this all the time.

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:56.769
It makes no sense.
-Yeah. Yeah, I love it.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:59.105
You go, snap, snap, snap.
We can all snap.

00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:01.107
[ Audience snapping fingers ]
That sounds good.

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:03.176
That means
you're destroying right now.

00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:08.013
So -- But I love how
it's one person starting change,

00:04:08.013 --> 00:04:10.116
and then the band slowly grows,

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:14.386
and there's doubters along
the way and there's haters.

00:04:14.386 --> 00:04:16.589
And they -- You change them
around, switch them around.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:20.225
By the end, you have this
beautiful band of kids

00:04:20.225 --> 00:04:22.127
all singing
and making change together.

00:04:22.127 --> 00:04:24.730
-They're like The Roots.
-They're kind of like -- Yeah.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.266
-Literally.
-They are like The Roots.

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:28.901
Yeah, they are like The Roots.

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:31.370
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:34.040
Would it be okay if I asked you
to read some of the book to us?

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.408
-Yes, of course.

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:37.877
-I don't know where you --
-Thank you.

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:39.044
I'll start.

00:04:39.044 --> 00:04:40.646
-Yeah, tell me
where you want to start.

00:04:40.646 --> 00:04:42.514
-This is kind of the middle.
-Okay.

00:04:42.514 --> 00:04:44.817
-"Though some
don't understand it,

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:47.386
those windmills of mysteries,

00:04:47.386 --> 00:04:53.326
I sing with all the planet
in its hills of histories.

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:56.295
I'm bright as the light
each day brings.

00:04:56.295 --> 00:04:58.998
There is love
where my change sings."

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:02.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:02.467 --> 00:05:05.872
-Every library, every school,
every kid, get this book.

00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:09.375
Amanda Gorman!
-Thank you.

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:11.744
"Change Sings:
A Children's Anthem,"

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:13.179
is available right now.

00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:14.881
And she will be president
one day.

